  "id": "DrugBank_DB13231 - MONDO_2319",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Lactate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Phosphorus Metabolism Disease through a series of indirect pathways involving cardiac arrest. The evidence suggests that Calcium Lactate is associated with Cardiac Arrest, which in turn is linked to other calcium salts such as Calcium Acetate and Calcium Glucoheptonate, all of which are indicated to contribute to Phosphorus Metabolism Disease. These relationships imply a potential role of calcium salts in disrupting normal metabolic processes related to phosphorus, possibly through effects on cellular function or ion homeostasis. The consistent association across different calcium salts highlights a possible mechanism where altered calcium levels may influence phosphorus metabolism, though the exact molecular pathways remain unclear. The evidence supports a network of interactions where calcium salts are linked to metabolic disturbances, suggesting a broader role in metabolic disorders.",
